.TH RESIZE_MXFS 8 "2026-03-10" "MXFS 0.10.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
resize_mxfs \- grow an MXFS filesystem to fill an expanded device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B resize_mxfs
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.I device
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B resize_mxfs
grows an MXFS filesystem after the underlying block device has been
expanded (e.g., after an iSCSI LUN resize or LVM
.BR lvextend (8)).
.PP
The tool appends new XFS allocation groups at the end of the XFS data
area and updates both the XFS and MXFS superblocks to reflect the new
geometry.  The MXFS superblock, journal, and disklock regions are at
fixed offsets at the start of the device and are never moved.
.PP
.B resize_mxfs
only supports growing.  Shrinking a filesystem is not supported.
.SS How Growing Works
.nf
Before:
  [MXFS reserved ~96MB] [XFS data (AG 0, AG 1)]
  0                      xfs_data_offset          old_dev_end

After:
  [MXFS reserved ~96MB] [XFS data (AG 0, AG 1, AG 2, AG 3, ...)]
  0                      xfs_data_offset                          new_dev_end
.fi
.SS New AG Structure
Each new allocation group contains 5 header blocks followed by free space:
.TP
.B Block 0
XFS superblock (secondary copy) + AGF + AGI + AGFL (4 sectors)
.TP
.B Block 1
BNO btree root (free space by block number, 1 record)
.TP
.B Block 2
CNT btree root (free space by size, 1 record)
.TP
.B Block 3
INO btree root (inode allocation, empty)
.TP
.B Block 4
FINO btree root (free inode tracking, empty)
.TP
.B Block 5+
Available as free space
.PP
Free blocks per new AG = AG size in blocks minus 5.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n
Dry run.  Display what would be done without writing any changes to
the device.  Useful for verifying resize geometry before committing.
.TP
.BR \-v
Verbose output.  Show detailed per-AG information during the resize.
.TP
.BR \-V
Print the version string and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Grow the filesystem after expanding the underlying LUN:
.PP
.RS
.nf
resize_mxfs /dev/sdb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Dry run to preview the resize plan:
.PP
.RS
.nf
resize_mxfs -n /dev/sdb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Typical workflow for expanding shared storage:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# 1. Expand the iSCSI LUN on the storage appliance
# 2. Rescan the device on all nodes:
iscsiadm -m session --rescan
# 3. Unmount on all nodes:
umount /mnt/shared
# 4. Resize on one node:
resize_mxfs /dev/sdb
# 5. Remount on all nodes:
mount -t mxfs /dev/sdb /mnt/shared
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Filesystem resized successfully, or dry run completed.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (device not larger, invalid superblock, I/O error, etc.).
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 3
The filesystem must be unmounted on all nodes before resizing.
.IP \(bu 3
The new device size must be strictly larger than the recorded device size
in the MXFS superblock.
.IP \(bu 3
A new allocation group requires at least 64 blocks (256 KB with 4 KB
blocks).  Any remainder smaller than 64 blocks is not used.
.IP \(bu 3
The tool writes new AG structures first, then updates the primary XFS
superblock, then updates secondary superblock copies in existing AGs,
and finally updates the MXFS superblock.  A crash during resize leaves
the old filesystem intact if the superblock updates have not yet been
written.
.IP \(bu 3
Multiple iterative resize operations are supported.  Each run adds new
AGs for whatever space is available.
.IP \(bu 3
Both block devices
.RB ( BLKGETSIZE64
ioctl) and regular files
.RB ( fstat (2))
are supported.
.IP \(bu 3
The tool is standalone with no libmxfs linkage.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/resize_mxfs
The resize tool binary.
